{
  "passage": "The Mughal emperors ruled a very large and varied empire, and they needed a system to manage it. Akbar, who ruled from 1556 to 1605, organised the administration with great care. He introduced the mansabdari system. A mansabdar was a person who held a mansab, meaning a rank or position in the emperor's service. Each mansabdar was given a rank called zat, which decided his position and salary. He also had to maintain a fixed number of horsemen, called sawar, for the army. Mansabdars were not paid in cash. Instead, they were given revenue assignments called jagirs, which allowed them to collect taxes from certain areas. Under Akbar, mansabdars did not live in their jagirs, and the emperor's officers made sure that the taxes collected were fair. To fix the land revenue, Akbar's revenue minister, Todar Mal, carried out a careful survey of crop yields, prices and the area cultivated over ten years. The tax was then fixed for each crop in cash. The empire was divided into provinces called subas, each governed by a subadar. Akbar also followed a policy called sulh-i kul, meaning universal peace, which did not discriminate between people of different religions. Abul Fazl, a close friend of Akbar, wrote a history of his reign called the Akbar Nama.",
